<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sacred Cenote</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cenote of Sacrifice</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sacred Cenote is a large natural sinkhole at Chichen Itza in Yucatán, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, linked to the city's main plaza by a raised causeway. The Maya threw offerings into it, including gold, jade, copal, pottery and human victims, as gifts to the rain god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaac">Chaac</a>, and pilgrims still visited it at the Spanish conquest. Dredging and diving since the early 20th century, beginning with Edward Thompson, have recovered thousands of objects.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sacred-cenote.jpg" alt="Sacred Cenote" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Salhedine · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cenote_Xtoloc_en_Chich%C3%A9n_Itz%C3%A1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
